  ('p', '[1] On a hot summer afternoon, the surface of a black rooftop in a large city can reach 70°C, while the air a few metres above it is only 33°C. Thousands of such roofs heat the surrounding air, making cities several degrees warmer than the countryside. This effect, known as the "urban heat island," increases the use of air conditioning and can be dangerous for elderly people.'),
  ('p', '[2] One solution that many cities are now trying is the green roof: a layer of soil and plants on top of a building. Plants cool the air in two ways. First, they shade the roof from direct sunlight. Second, they release water vapour from their leaves, a process that takes heat from the surroundings, much as sweat cools our skin.'),
